shaderc: GLSL ES fragment shaders compute in full precision

SPIRV-Cross writes `precision mediump float;` at the top of every GLSL ES fragment shader unless told otherwise, and
shaderc never told it: every local and varying of a fragment shader was mediump. Desktop GPUs run mediump at full
precision, but mobile GPUs (Apple, Mali, Adreno) may compute it in 16-bit floats: HDR lighting overflows past 65504
and positions rebuilt from depth lose their precision. Fragment shaders now default to highp, which OpenGL ES 3.0
requires every fragment shader to support, and compute in 32-bit floats as the other shader languages bgfx writes do.

diff --git a/tools/shaderc/shaderc_glsl.cpp b/tools/shaderc/shaderc_glsl.cpp
--- a/tools/shaderc/shaderc_glsl.cpp
+++ b/tools/shaderc/shaderc_glsl.cpp
@@ -226,6 +226,8 @@ namespace bgfx { namespace glsl
 			compilerOptions.vulkan_semantics                   = false;
 			compilerOptions.enable_420pack_extension           = false;
 			compilerOptions.emit_uniform_buffer_as_plain_uniforms = true;
+			// GLSL ES fragment shaders default to mediump, which mobile GPUs compute in 16-bit floats.
+			compilerOptions.fragment.default_float_precision   = spirv_cross::CompilerGLSL::Options::Highp;
 			compiler.set_common_options(compilerOptions);
 
 			spirv_cross::ShaderResources resources = compiler.get_shader_resources();
